  "broker_policy_text": "SYNTHETIC DATA \u2014 requires reviewer sign-off\n\n# Paratransit broker \u2014 dispatch policy\n\nDialysis riders travel on standing orders. Each treatment day the outbound leg is\nbooked to arrive before the chair start, and the return leg is anchored thirty\nminutes after the scheduled session end.\n\nEvery trip gets a thirty-minute pickup window. The vehicle is on time if it arrives\nanywhere inside that window; arriving outside it is a late trip and is recorded.\n\nWe may negotiate a requested time by up to sixty minutes either side. That is the\nwhole of our discretion. A trip that cannot be placed inside that band is not\nscheduled; it is queued as a denial and the reason is logged for the record.\n\nNo vehicle picks anyone up before 05:30, when the fleet's shift starts.\n\nA driver waits five minutes at the curb. After that the vehicle continues and the\nrider is put back into the queue rather than left waiting for a vehicle that has gone.\n\nCapacity is counted by mobility class at every stop, not per trip. A standard van\ncarries four ambulatory riders and one wheelchair. A lift van carries three\nambulatory riders and two wheelchairs. Neither carries a stretcher.\n\nStretcher trips are never auto-assigned. They go to a dispatcher, who books the\ncrew and the equipment by hand.\n\nNo rider spends more than sixty minutes on board, and no ride is longer than twice\nthe direct trip. If a shared route would break that, the pairing is dropped.\n\nNobody is stranded. Every return leg is either scheduled or sitting in the review\nqueue on will-call, and a will-call is a promise to come, not a cancellation.\n\nVehicles are on the road from 05:30 to 21:30. Outside those hours there is no service.\n",
